1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Why Domain Matters, part 2: Logistics and supply chain. The record says delivered on time, in full; one pallet line held the wrong pack size.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,800
Part 2 follows one delivery through the people who see it differently.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:16,200
The same synthetic world: Store 014 in Jayanagar, and the warehouse in Hoskote that supplies it.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:23,600
Every record of line 1 says 5 kg. The pallet held 160 packs of 1 kg. The scan confirmed the slot, not the item.

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:30,000
The books said 63. The shelf was empty for 51.5 open hours, and nothing was reordered.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,400
Four corrections, two buildings, one mistake. They net to about ₹80, so nobody chases them.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:42,800
OTIF for June: 92%, 90%, 88% or 84%, depending on whose record decides.

8
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:49,200
The demo replays June with the same customers and trucks. Change how the warehouse checks, or how the store receives.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:55,600
The DC shift supervisor sees every line confirmed, and two count variances, both corrected.

10
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:01,000
The receiving clerk sees pallet labels that matched the notice. No discrepancies.

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,400
The scorecard sees 88% for June, with 22 June on time and in full.

12
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:13,800
The stock controller sees 63 bags of 5 kg gone and 157 packs of 1 kg from nowhere.

13
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:21,200
What actually happened: one mis-pick, 51.5 open hours with an empty shelf, 72 bags not sold.

14
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:27,600
Scan every case at pick: the mistake never happens. OTIF stays 88%, and now it is true.

15
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:34,000
Weigh every pallet: caught at dispatch, the truck arrives late. OTIF falls to 84%; the shelf stays full.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,400
Scan every case at the store: caught on arrival. OTIF falls to 84% again, because it now counts what arrived.

17
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:47,800
The records behind it. Nothing says “wrong item”. Download them and find the line yourself.

18
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:54,200
The post ends with a learning path: warehouse science, GS1 identifiers, India’s e-way bill, and practice on June’s records.

19
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:59,700
Every record agreed. That is exactly why nobody checked the pallet.
